### Why does volatile counter++ fail?

```java
package com.bank.core;

public class VolatileCounterFail {
    private static volatile int counter = 0;

    public static void main(String[] args) throws InterruptedException {
        Thread t1 = new Thread(() -> {
            for (int i = 0; i < 100000; i++) counter++;
        });
        Thread t2 = new Thread(() -> {
            for (int i = 0; i < 100000; i++) counter++;
        });
        t1.start(); t2.start();
        t1.join();  t2.join();
        System.out.println("Volatile counter: " + counter);
    }
}
```

```text
Volatile counter: 163847
```

Expected 200,000 but got less because `counter++` is three operations and threads interleave.

### How does AtomicInteger fix this?

```java
package com.bank.core;

import java.util.concurrent.atomic.AtomicInteger;

public class AtomicCounterFix {
    private static final AtomicInteger counter = new AtomicInteger(0);

    public static void main(String[] args) throws InterruptedException {
        Thread t1 = new Thread(() -> {
            for (int i = 0; i < 100000; i++) counter.incrementAndGet();
        });
        Thread t2 = new Thread(() -> {
            for (int i = 0; i < 100000; i++) counter.incrementAndGet();
        });
        t1.start(); t2.start();
        t1.join();  t2.join();
        System.out.println("Atomic counter: " + counter.get());
    }
}
```

```text
Atomic counter: 200000
```

### How does CAS work inside AtomicInteger?

```java
// Simplified version of AtomicInteger.incrementAndGet():
public int incrementAndGet() {
    int prev, next;
    do {
        prev = get();           // volatile read: current value
        next = prev + 1;        // compute new value
    } while (!compareAndSet(prev, next)); // CAS: set to next ONLY IF still prev
    return next;
}
// If another thread changed the value between get() and compareAndSet(),
// CAS returns false and the loop retries with the fresh value.
```

### When should you use LongAdder instead?

Under high contention (many threads incrementing the same counter), CAS retries increase. LongAdder uses per-thread cells to eliminate contention.

```java
package com.bank.core;

import java.util.concurrent.atomic.LongAdder;

public class LongAdderExample {
    private static final LongAdder counter = new LongAdder();

    public static void main(String[] args) throws InterruptedException {
        Thread t1 = new Thread(() -> {
            for (int i = 0; i < 100000; i++) counter.increment();
        });
        Thread t2 = new Thread(() -> {
            for (int i = 0; i < 100000; i++) counter.increment();
        });
        t1.start(); t2.start();
        t1.join();  t2.join();
        System.out.println("LongAdder: " + counter.sum());
    }
}
```

```text
LongAdder: 200000
```

### How do the approaches compare?

| Approach | Thread-safe? | Performance (64 threads) | Use when |
|---|---|---|---|
| `volatile int; counter++` | No | N/A (broken) | Never for counters |
| `synchronized { counter++ }` | Yes | Slowest (lock contention) | Simple, low throughput |
| `AtomicInteger.incrementAndGet()` | Yes | Fast (CAS retries) | Low-medium contention |
| `LongAdder.increment()` | Yes | Fastest (per-thread cells) | High contention (64+ threads) |

### What should a senior engineer know?

- **LongAdder trade-off**: `sum()` is eventually consistent during concurrent updates. It returns the sum of all cells, but in-flight increments may not be reflected. This is fine for metrics but not for exact real-time counts.
- **LongAccumulator**: generalized version of LongAdder that accepts a custom accumulator function (e.g., max, min, sum).
- **DoubleAdder**: same striped design for floating-point accumulation.
